  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 408 AM CDT Sat Aug 15 2026\n\nSurface trough axis remains draped across KS with a stationary \nboundary holding across the center of the CWA. At the upper levels, \nwe remain on the northwest periphery of the ridge dominating the \nDeep South while a trough is noted over the West Coast states. The \nnose of the LLJ has pushed north of the KS/NE border and helped \nsteer the storms that began late last night into the MO/IA/IL \nvicinity. A couple of other batches of storms are making their way \nthrough western KS and NE, and a convectively induced vort max is \nnoted in that area on water vapor imagery. Some showers and weak \nstorms left over from that activity may survive into north central \nKS this morning before dissipating around mid-day. How far south any \nprecip and cloud debris ends up as a result of this leads to some \nquestions on how hot temperatures will actually get today, \nparticularly in counties along the KS River Valley and northward. If \nthe sfc boundary hangs near its current location, clouds clear out \nsoon enough, and southerly winds pick up enough, then most locations \nwithin the Extreme Heat Warning should be able to heat up and see \nheat indices in the 105 to 110 degree range. However, if the \nboundary sags south during the day and/or cloud cover lingers \nlonger, then it's possible even locations along I-70 could struggle \nto reach heat indices of 105. Areas along and south of the Turnpike \nhave the best chance of getting there as it stands now. Given that \nsome hi-res guidance does still show potential for hotter \ntemperatures with a northerly placement of the front, have gone with \nmore of a middle ground solution on temperatures and left the \nwarning as is for now. \n\nRegarding storm chances tonight, there are a couple of opportunities \nto watch. One is with convergence along the front by early evening, \nalthough forcing looks rather weak and coverage looks isolated and \nshort-lived. Still, if something can get going, inverted-V soundings \nwould support strong wind gusts. The better chance for storms looks \nto come from another batch of convection that begins in the higher \nterrain and eventually makes it into north central KS overnight \n(probably after 1-2am). It remains uncertain how strong these storms \nwould still be by the time they reach this area, but MUCAPE around \n1500 J/kg and modest bulk shear could support a few marginally \nsevere storms.\n\nAny storm activity should move out Sunday morning with the front \nfinally pushing south by then as well. This leaves us with cooler \ntemperatures as highs are in the 80s, perhaps a few low 90s in our \nfar southeast. Unfortunately, dew points won't be much better as \nthey are stuck in the 70s, but the lower temperatures will still \nmake it much more manageable. Monday looks similar as all areas \nshould see highs in the 80s.\n\nFor the middle and especially end of next week, we transition to \nquasi-zonal to northwesterly flow aloft with the ridge pushed to our \nsouth. Although temperatures warm up slightly Tuesday with southerly \nlow-level flow, the bulk of the heat should stay south of the area. \nPerturbations embedded in the upper flow with the low-level \nbaroclinic zone nearby result in greater uncertainty with \ntemperatures mid to late week. Rain chances look to be mostly during \nthe overnight periods Tuesday night and Wednesday night, but timing \nand placement of those waves should come into better focus as we get \ncloser to each day.",
